{"content_hash": "9eae657fd2368416", "version_id": 276, "article_id": 28, "level_code": "ja_n5", "lang": "ja", "level": 1, "level_label": "N5 初級", "title": "世界でけがが多い　病院のやり方をかえる時", "title_zh": "全球外伤问题严重，医院该改改做法了", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-global-injury-crisis-highlights-urgent.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49492358448_70ce40b0d5_b.jpg", "credit": "NavyMedicine · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 314, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "世界には、けがで死ぬ人がたくさんいます。新しい研究が、アフリカと南アジアの病院をしらべました。", "lead_zh": "全世界有很多人因为受伤而死亡。一项新研究调查了非洲和南亚的医院。", "preview": "研究は、アフリカと南アジアの四つの国でしました。国はガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカです。十九の病院で、八千八百五十八人の人をしらべました。", "preview_zh": "研究在非洲和南亚的四个国家进行。这四个国家是加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非。研究调查了19家医院里的8858个人。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 266, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@cec1515/content/audio/28/ja_n5.mp3", "duration": 71.338, "size_bytes": 431712, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 213, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@cec1515/content/data/versions/276.json", "paragraphs": ["研究は、アフリカと南アジアの四つの国でしました。国はガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカです。十九の病院で、八千八百五十八人の人をしらべました。", "三か月後、けがをした人の半分近くが、死んだか、体が不自由になりました。病院で死んだ人は6.1パーセントです。三か月で死んだ人は9.7パーセントです。体が不自由な人は40.6パーセントです。", "びっくりしたことに、救急車で来た人の方が、悪くなりました。でも、これは救急車が悪いという意味ではありません。", "研究者は言います。お金のある国のやり方を、そのままコピーしてはだめです。その国に合うやり方を、自分で考えることが大切です。", "けがで死ぬ人の90パーセントは、お金の少ない国にいます。しかし、けがの治療は、あまり注目されていません。もっと研究とデータが必要です。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["研究在非洲和南亚的四个国家进行。这四个国家是加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非。研究调查了19家医院里的8858个人。", "三个月后，受伤的人里将近一半要么死了，要么身体留下了残疾。在医院里死亡的人占6.1%。三个月内死亡的人占9.7%。留下中重度残疾的人占40.6%。", "让人意外的是，坐救护车来的人情况反而更糟。但这并不是说救护车不好。", "研究者说：不能直接照搬富裕国家的做法。重要的是根据自己国家的情况，想出自己的办法。", "因受伤而死亡的人里，90%生活在低收入国家。可是外伤治疗并没有得到多少关注。还需要更多的研究和数据。"], "body": "研究は、アフリカと南アジアの四つの国でしました。国はガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカです。十九の病院で、八千八百五十八人の人をしらべました。\n\n三か月後、けがをした人の半分近くが、死んだか、体が不自由になりました。病院で死んだ人は6.1パーセントです。三か月で死んだ人は9.7パーセントです。体が不自由な人は40.6パーセントです。\n\nびっくりしたことに、救急車で来た人の方が、悪くなりました。でも、これは救急車が悪いという意味ではありません。\n\n研究者は言います。お金のある国のやり方を、そのままコピーしてはだめです。その国に合うやり方を、自分で考えることが大切です。\n\nけがで死ぬ人の90パーセントは、お金の少ない国にいます。しかし、けがの治療は、あまり注目されていません。もっと研究とデータが必要です。", "body_zh": "研究在非洲和南亚的四个国家进行。这四个国家是加纳、巴基斯坦、卢旺达和南非。研究调查了19家医院里的8858个人。\n\n三个月后，受伤的人里将近一半要么死了，要么身体留下了残疾。在医院里死亡的人占6.1%。三个月内死亡的人占9.7%。留下中重度残疾的人占40.6%。\n\n让人意外的是，坐救护车来的人情况反而更糟。但这并不是说救护车不好。\n\n研究者说：不能直接照搬富裕国家的做法。重要的是根据自己国家的情况，想出自己的办法。\n\n因受伤而死亡的人里，90%生活在低收入国家。可是外伤治疗并没有得到多少关注。还需要更多的研究和数据。", "text": "研究は、アフリカと南アジアの四つの国でしました。国はガーナ、パキスタン、ルワンダ、南アフリカです。十九の病院で、八千八百五十八人の人をしらべました。 三か月後、けがをした人の半分近くが、死んだか、体が不自由になりました。病院で死んだ人は6.1パーセントです。三か月で死んだ人は9.7パーセントです。体が不自由な人は40.6パーセントです。 びっくりしたことに、救急車で来た人の方が、悪くなりました。でも、これは救急車が悪いという意味ではありません。 研究者は言います。お金のある国のやり方を、そのままコピーしてはだめです。その国に合うやり方を、自分で考えることが大切です。 けがで死ぬ人の90パーセントは、お金の少ない国にいます。しかし、けがの治療は、あまり注目されていません。もっと研究とデータが必要です。", "vocab": [{"word": "けが", "pos": "noun", "zh": "受伤", "note": "日常常用词"}, {"word": "病院", "pos": "noun", "zh": "医院", "note": "基本名词"}, {"word": "死ぬ", "pos": "verb", "zh": "死", "note": "五段动词"}, {"word": "人", "pos": "noun", "zh": "人", "note": "基本名词"}, {"word": "研究", "pos": "noun", "zh": "研究", "note": "新闻常见词"}, {"word": "三か月", "pos": "noun", "zh": "三个月", "note": "时间表达"}, {"word": "救急車", "pos": "noun", "zh": "救护车", "note": "生活相关词"}, {"word": "大切", "pos": "adj", "zh": "重要", "note": "な形容词"}], "title_original": "Global injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care", "summary_original": "A study of almost 9,000 injury patients across Africa and South Asia has found that nearly half were dead or disabled after three months—highlighting the need for smarter, locally tailored trauma-care systems.", "body_original": "Global injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care\nby\nUniversity of Birmingham\nedited by\nSadie Harley\n, reviewed by\nAndrew Zinin\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\npeer-reviewed publication\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nA study of almost 9,000 injury patients across Africa and South Asia has found that nearly half were dead or disabled after three months—highlighting the need for smarter, locally tailored trauma-care systems.\nPublishing its\nfindings in\nThe Lancet Global Health\n, the international research group led by University of Birmingham experts reveals that the biggest influences on survival and recovery are not just the injuries themselves, but how people move through the health care system.\nInjuries cause an estimated 4.4 million deaths annually worldwide, with 90% occurring in LMICs. This is the first large multicountry study to examine mortality and disability outcomes among hospitalized injury patients across low- or middle-income countries (LMIC).\nThe study reveals that access to appropriate care, the route patients take to the hospital and the type of services available all play an important role in these outcomes.\nAnalyzing data from 8,858 patients treated in 19 urban and rural hospitals across Ghana, Pakistan, Rwanda and South Africa, the Global Health Group on Equitable Access to Quality Health Care for Injured People in Four Low- or Middle-Income Countries (Equi-injury) reveals an enormous and often overlooked global health burden:\n47.6% of patients either died or were left with moderate-to-severe disability within three months of hospital discharge.\n6.1% died in the hospital, 9.7% had died within three months, and 40.6% were living with moderate-to-severe disability three months after discharge.\nPatients arriving at the hospital by ambulance had significantly worse outcomes, with 86% higher odds of death or disability and more than double the odds of mortality within three months compared with those using other forms of transportation.\nLead author Professor Justine Davies, from the University of Birmingham, said, \"One of our most surprising findings was that measures often assumed to improve outcomes—such as ambulance transport and rapid transfer to major hospitals, which are similar to highly centralized trauma services—were not consistently linked with better recovery or survival.\n\"This does not mean that ambulances cause worse outcomes but suggests that investment in ambulance systems alone is unlikely to improve outcomes unless services are coordinated and appropriately resourced.\"\nThe findings challenge assumptions that health care models developed in wealthier countries can simply be copied elsewhere. Instead, the study argues that countries should develop trauma and injury-care systems based on local evidence, resources and patient needs.\nCorresponding author Dr. Leila Ghalichi, from the University of Birmingham, said, \"This does not mean ambulances or specialist trauma centers are ineffective. Rather, it suggests that simply investing in expensive emergency systems is not enough.\n\"LMICs should be cautious about investing heavily in ambulance services and specialist, centralized trauma centers without ensuring they are well coordinated, properly staffed and supported by strong health systems. Better data and further research are needed to understand which trauma-care investments improve patient outcomes.\"\nThe study notes that stopping first at a nearby hospital for emergency stabilization may sometimes be beneficial, especially if a specialist hospital is far away. If a major trauma hospital is close by, going directly there may improve outcomes.\nResearchers found no evidence that reaching a hospital more quickly reduced the risk of death. Patients who experienced some delays were less likely to be disabled later—challenging the common belief that faster treatment always leads to better outcomes after injury.\nAge was linked to worse outcomes, but gender, wealth and education had little effect on survival while patients were in the hospital. However, once out of the hospital, wealthier and better-educated patients tended to recover better, suggesting that access to rehabilitation and follow-up care may play an important role.\nSome 90% of global injury deaths occur in LMICs and are estimated to account for around 10% of the global burden of disease. Yet trauma care receives far less attention than infectious diseases or chronic conditions in global health policy.\nCountry-level differences were striking. Patients in South Africa had the lowest odds of disability but the highest odds of mortality, while patients in Rwanda experienced the lowest odds of mortality. Meanwhile, patients in Pakistan experienced substantially higher odds of the combined outcome of death or disability than those in Ghana.\nThe study population reflects the demographics of injury worldwide. The median age was 31 years, 76.8% were male, and road traffic collisions accounted for 50% of all injuries recorded. Orthopedic injuries were the most common injury type, affecting 39.2% of patients.\nPublication details\nJustine Davies et al, Mortality and disability after hospitalisation for injuries in low-income and middle-income countries: a cohort study,\nThe Lancet Global Health\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1016/j.langlo.2026.104053\nJournal information:\nThe Lancet Global Health\nKey medical concepts\nInjury\nMortality\nClinical categories\nEmergency medicine\nHospital medicine\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nProvided by\nUniversity of Birmingham\nWho's behind this story?\nSadie Harley\nBSc Life Sciences & Ecology. Microbiology lab background with pharmaceutical news experience in oil, gas, and renewable industries.\nFull profile →\nAndrew Zinin\nMaster's in physics with research experience. Long-time science news enthusiast. Plays key role in Science X's editorial success.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nGlobal injury crisis highlights urgent need for smarter trauma care (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-global-injury-crisis-highlights-urgent.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T12:35:25.451601+00:00", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "研究", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.625, "sm": 125, "em": 625, "cs": 0, "ce": 2, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "は", "s": 0.637, "e": 0.9, "sm": 637, "em": 899, "cs": 2, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "、", "s": 0.9, "e": 1.188, "sm": 899, "em": 1187, "cs": 3, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "アフリカ", "s": 1.188, "e": 1.7, "sm": 1187, "em": 1700, "cs": 4, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "と", "s": 1.712, "e": 1.837, "sm": 1712, "em": 1837, "cs": 8, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "南", "s": 1.85, "e": 2.225, "sm": 1850, "em": 2225, "cs": 9, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "アジア", "s": 2.237, "e": 2.55, "sm": 2237, "em": 2550, "cs": 10, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "の", "s": 2.562, "e": 2.688, "sm": 2562, "em": 2687, "cs": 13, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "四つ", "s": 2.7, "e": 3.05, "sm": 2700, "em": 3050, "cs": 14, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "の", "s": 3.062, "e": 3.175, "sm": 3062, "em": 3175, "cs": 16, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "国", "s": 3.188, "e": 3.413, "sm": 3187, "em": 3412, "cs": 17, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "でし", "s": 3.425, "e": 3.662, "sm": 3425, "em": 3662, "cs": 18, "ce": 20, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "まし", "s": 3.675, "e": 3.9, "sm": 3675, "em": 3900, "cs": 20, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 13, "w": "た", "s": 3.913, "e": 4.138, "sm": 3912, "em": 4137, "cs": 22, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 14, "w": "。", "s": 4.138, "e": 5.062, "sm": 4137, "em": 5062, "cs": 23, "ce": 24, "si": 0}, {"i": 15, "w": "国", "s": 5.062, "e": 5.275, "sm": 5062, "em": 5275, "cs": 24, "ce": 25, "si": 1}, {"i": 16, "w": 